<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rumi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1207">1207</a>–<a href="/years/1273">1273</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Jalal al-Din Rumi, Mawlana, Mevlana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rumi, Jalal al-Din Muhammad, was a Persian poet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic and scholar born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in 1207. His family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, capital of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, in the 1220s, and he taught there until his death in 1273. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a> and lyric poems are among the greatest works of Persian literature, and his followers founded the Mevlevi order of whirling dervishes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rumi.jpg" alt="Rumi" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hossein Behzad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%88%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%A7_%D8%A7%D8%AB%D8%B1_%D8%AD%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%AF_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court followed Sunni Islam, but Sufi teachers and dervishes found a welcome, and in the 1220s the family of the mystic <b>Jalal al-Din Rumi</b> settled in Konya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ibn Ishaq in the 8th century; works of theology and philosophy; and the poetry and teaching of the Sufis, from the treatises of al-Ghazali, whose Revival of the Religious Sciences reconciled law and mysticism around 1100, to the Persian poetry of <b>Jalal al-Din Rumi</b> in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian spread to Anatolia, where the Seljuk sultans of Rum hosted <b>Rumi</b>, and Ottoman Turkish absorbed a large Persian vocabulary.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vakhsh, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.71,68.83&amp;year=1249" aria-label="Vakhsh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6268 807 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rumi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6718 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6718 1077h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.71,68.83&amp;year=1249">What was here in 1249? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Love is the astrolabe of God's mysteries. A lover may hanker after this love or that love, But at the last he is drawn to the KING of Love.&#8221;</p><footer>Masnavi, book 1 &#183; tr. E. H. Whinfield, 1887 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every moment the voice of Love is coming from left and right. We are bound for heaven: who has a mind to sight-seeing?&#8221;</p><footer>Divan-i Shams-i Tabrizi &#183; tr. R. A. Nicholson, 1898 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The sea itself is one thing, the foam another; Neglect the foam, and regard the sea with your eyes.&#8221;</p><footer>Masnavi &#183; tr. E. H. Whinfield, 1887 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whoso recognises and confesses his own defects Is hastening in the way that leads to Perfection! But he advances not towards the Almighty Who fancies himself to be perfect.&#8221;</p><footer>Masnavi &#183; tr. E. H. Whinfield, 1887 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The sect of lovers is distinct from all others, Lovers have a religion and a faith of their own. Though the ruby has no stamp, what matters it? Love is fearless in the midst of the sea of fear.&#8221;</p><footer>Masnavi &#183; tr. E. H. Whinfield, 1887 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Pain is a treasure, for it contains mercies; The kernel is soft when the rind is scraped off.&#8221;</p><footer>Masnavi &#183; tr. E. H. Whinfield, 1887 (in F. Hadland Davis, The Persian Mystics: Rumi, 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45159/pg45159-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
